hat that hundred bytes that is being  used for the email is also in every  subsequent index as a lookup  which means that they had 10x the amount  of data they needed to store  now what we ended up doing was  converting their email primary key to a  auto increment so a sequence a number  one two three four five six and we also  put a hash  crc32 hash of the email address and we  made those two combined their primary  key that minute was a maximum of eight  bytes as opposed to the hundred so there  was a 10x improvement not only in  storage but there was a massive almost  20x improvement in performance  because  less data is in memory  less  uh cpu cycles are spent trying to  compare  the uh  varchar values and you know uh it was a  huge win for them  so many of you might use uuids and many  orms you use use uuids internally so a  32 you know by representative uh of a  uni uh unique uh id  that can actually kill performance and  so that's something to keep in mind  now i mentioned that you know mysql  the indexes all contain the primary key  indexes are probably your number one  friend when it comes to troubleshooting  queries and query performance  and so you've probably heard this  numerous times all over the world so i'm  not going to go too much into this but i  want to leave you with a couple of  things too much of a good thing can be a  bad thing  i've seen it many many times where  people will index almost every column  that is not good okay you should limit  the number of indexes because every  index you add adds additional overhead  to maintain that index  so every column doesn't need an index  just the most frequently queried or  filtered columns do every database has  their own set of indexes and there's  different use cases for individual  indexes now we've done talks before i'm  happy to point you to some resources if  you're interested in which individual  indexes do what  we do have that  detail there  now i want to also say  use what you need okay  don't keep everything just in case  archive what you don't need and be  mindful that a lot of orms and a lot of  database applications do a select star  to select all the data from the uh  systems all at once this is something  that can kill performance okay  i'm sorry to interrupt you we've hit uh  the time limit for talk so we should  slowly move uh to the  question uh answers and questions so  please uh finish the idea and slowly  move to the okay i think i only have  like two more slides so  one minute um so be mindful of that  select star um and i do have some  uh configuration options in case you are  interested mysql configuration options  which ones you should uh potentially  look at and postgres configuration  options uh but keep in mind you have to  tune your system for the workload  and um  don't over engineer things if possible  uh you could follow us if you want to  get more on this topic we have a vibrant  youtube channel we also have a blog with  uh you know tons of 15 years worth of  database content all in the open source  space
 

